ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186445677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186479902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124593 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1045 "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1050 1320.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124728 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "76681192-0bc8-5fd9-98fc-5fd254bc93fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+e\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f10e177e-2926-5cde-8e9a-4b0a99b2ff4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124959 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+yz+xz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+yz+xz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+yz+xz)\\,k \\\\  & =(y+z)\\,i+(x+z)\\,j+(y+x)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y={e}^{t},$ and $z=e$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e)\\,k \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}+e)\\cdot 1+(t+e)\\cdot {e}^{t}+({e}^{t}+t)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+e+t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+3xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t+1,-t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e234e1ce-e3a5-5460-9b85-a8876cb6e36c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124960 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+3xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x+3yz,3xz,3xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=t+1,$ and $z=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2({t}^{2})+3(t+1)(-t),3({t}^{2})(-t),3({t}^{2})(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}-3{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-{t}^{2}-3t)\\cdot 2t+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-8{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =-8{(1)}^{3}-9{(1)}^{2}=-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36253" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124961 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124961 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},4t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8571bcaa-6cdf-5385-98c4-d1ed2624cfd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124961 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{1}-4\\cdot 1)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125246 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+t\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72c15c65-cd14-5a6c-9b4a-a63cd2f705de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125246 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125250 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186124100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125250 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125250 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125250 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=z(x+y)$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125250 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9127ceaf-3e13-50a4-b08a-89114324036d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125250 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z(x+y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z(x+y))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z(x+y))\\,k \\\\  & =z\\,i+z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1061 12678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f62c8fc-daae-562a-a01e-851a375ec7a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1061 12681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ea045ecb-90e1-5dc8-804d-711207e5f86c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125319 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1061 12682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ef0f7fd7-782e-55cb-8008-f28d770450ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125320 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1061 12684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cde1eff3-797d-5f65-a385-6296a6f32db3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125321 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1061 30935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87b55278-4bb6-5686-b4ef-178d5036b236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125528 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1061 30936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4850b0cc-54ab-5a26-9440-b0d1cf757680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125529 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1061 30937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd19c878-fabf-5b99-9507-9a32fb862324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125530 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1064 "We can consider $f(\\mathbf{r}(t))$ as the values taken by the function $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$ So, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))]\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the **rate of change of $f$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t).$**\n\nFurthermore, if $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is a level curve $f(x,y)=c,$ then by the chain rule we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =c \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(f(r(t))) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(c) \\\\ ∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can conclude that the gradient vector $\\nabla f$ is orthogonal to every level curve of $f.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1065 2652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06350cc4-9c76-53db-a464-43e93f2fcc2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125749 #edb/ref 1067 "The Derivative as a Rate of Change" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the function \n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{r}) = f(x,y) = xy^2,\n$$\n\nwhich takes a vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t) \\right\\rangle = \\left\\langle \\cos t, \\sin t \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nas input. How do we calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right]?$\n\nTo do this, we use the **multivariable chain rule in vector form**, which states\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}\\left[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\right] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now apply this to our functions. First, we compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(f(x,y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(f(x,y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{y}^{2},2xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2},2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =-{sin}^{3}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1065 4967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "673ff670-99f1-5416-aa83-5c4c8bf0e4f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125835 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule for a function $f(\\mathbf{r}),$ where $\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x_1(t), x_2(t), \\ldots, x_m(t) \\right\\rangle$ is a vector-valued function of $m$ variables, is the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)\n$$\n\nHow does this chain rule in vector form relate to the chain rule we have seen before, which states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d f} {\\textrm d t} \t= \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_1}{\\textrm d t}  \n\t\t\t\t\t\t+ \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_2}{\\textrm d t} + \\dots + \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot  \\dfrac {\\textrm d x_m}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet's expand $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t)$ to see. Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))=⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}},\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}},…,\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{dt},\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{dt},…,\\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{dt}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{1}}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{2}}{\\text{d}t}+⋯+\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{x}_{m}}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey are exactly the same, just different notations!\n\n**Note**: If we replace $\\mathbf{r}$ with a single-valued function $g(t),$ we get back the chain rule from single-variable calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(g(t))] & =∇f(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1065 4968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "15062cce-eede-545a-ae5d-e02432a0ba33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125836 #edb/ref 1067 "Intuition Behind the Vector Form of the Multivariable Chain Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357945 #edb/ref 1080 "2-\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357945 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357945 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15d810bf-905d-5d78-ba48-12ddc9b892e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358135 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a6ad6c1-0c8d-5fb0-b435-dee857f73211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358819 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186358819 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 